25.5 Gain postings
==================

Each disposal transaction will have a balanced pair of postings
recording the capital gain (or loss):

   * *realised gain* (rgain) on a Gain-type account - the gain/loss,
     negated
   * *unrealised gain* (ugain) on an UnrealisedGain-type account - same
     amount, opposite sign

   Conceptually, the market value of a lot fluctuates from acquisition
onward, accumulating unrealised gain or loss.  At disposal, that
accumulated unrealised gain is reclassified as realised gain.

   Gain postings can be left implicit (and hledger will infer them); or
they can be written explicitly in the journal.  Recording gains below
shows examples of both.

   In inferred gain postings, the gain amount will be rounded to the
entry's local precision for the gain commodity (or if the local
precision is zero, two decimal digits will be shown - except when both
of those digits are zero).  More decimals can be seen by increasing the
display precision (eg 'hledger print --round=soft -c '$1.0000'').

   In explicit gain postings, the amount must also be written.  (Usually
hledger fills in missing amounts, but not in gain postings.)

* Menu:

* Gain and UnrealisedGain accounts::


File: hledger.info,  Node: Gain and UnrealisedGain accounts,  Up: Gain postings

25.5.1 Gain and UnrealisedGain accounts
---------------------------------------

The *'Gain'* (*'G'*) and *'UnrealisedGain'* (*'U'*) account types are
subtypes of Revenue and Equity respectively.  Declaring an account with
the G type (and optionally one with the U type) serves three purposes:

  1. *Recognising gain postings* - explicit gain postings which use the
     G account will be detected reliably by account type, and error
     messages may be clearer.
  2. *Customising account names* - inferred gain postings will use the
     first-declared G and U accounts, rather than the defaults
     ('revenues:gain' and 'equity:unrealised-gain').
  3. *Categorising* - when reporting, you can match on the G (or U)
     account type specifically.

   G and U are inferred from conventional English account names (eg
'revenues:gain', 'income:capital-gains', 'equity:unrealised-gain',
'equity:unrealized gains'; see the regex table under Account types).
You can also declare them explicitly:

account revenues:capital gain   ; type: G
account equity:unrealised gain  ; type: U

   Why do we post both gains and losses to a revenue account ?  It's
more convenient than using separate revenue and expense accounts, and
the sign keeps things correct.


File: hledger.info,  Node: Recording gains,  Next: Lot postings and balance assertions,  Prev: Gain postings,  Up: Lot reporting

25.6 Recording gains
====================

In the journal, you can write disposal transactions in four main styles,
with different tradeoffs between brevity, error checking, and
robustness.  From least to most explicit, they are:

   * *Style 1:* Don't write gain postings (let hledger infer them)
   * *Style 2:* Write the rgain posting, using a non-G account
     (undeclared gain posting)
   * *Style 3:* Write the rgain posting, using a G account (declared
     gain posting, more reliable)
   * *Style 4:* Write both rgain and ugain postings, using G/U accounts
     (most explicit)

   Style 1 just calculates the gain.  Styles 2-4 calculate the gain and
compare it with what you wrote, potentially catching more errors.

   *TLDR:* use Style 1 for brevity and to avoid most problems, or Style
3 for best error checking while still being concise.

* Menu:

* Style 1 No gain postings::
* Style 2 rgain posting non-G account::
* Style 3 rgain posting G account::
* Style 4 rgain and ugain postings G and U accounts::
* Gain postings and the roi command::


