25.6.1 Style 1: No gain postings
--------------------------------

2026-02-01 sell
    assets:stocks   -1 AAPL {$50} @ $60
    assets:cash     $60


File: hledger.info,  Node: Style 2 rgain posting non-G account,  Next: Style 3 rgain posting G account,  Prev: Style 1 No gain postings,  Up: Recording gains

25.6.2 Style 2: rgain posting, non-G account
--------------------------------------------

2026-02-01 sell
    assets:stocks     -1 AAPL {$50} @ $60
    assets:cash      $60
    gains           $-10

   Another example, this time with multiple gain postings:

2026-02-01 sell from two lots
    assets:stocks     -5 AAPL {$50} @ $60
    assets:stocks     -3 AAPL {$55} @ $60
    assets:cash     $480
    gains           $-50          ; gain on first lot
    gains           $-15          ; gain on second lot

   hledger identifies these "undeclared" gain postings by their
characteristics: postings whose account type is not Asset, Liability, or
Equity (or a subtype); and which have not been classified as lot
postings (acquire, transfer, dispose); and without which the remaining
postings in the entry balance to zero.

   So above, the 'gains' postings are detected, their amounts are
checked against the calculated value, and an unrealised gain posting is
added to balance them.  Any other imbalance - eg a typo in non-gain
postings -- is reported as a standard balance error, not silently
absorbed as gain.

   This heuristic detection of gain postings can fail (eg when the entry
has an undeclared gain posting and also a fee posting like
'expenses:fees').  You can check it with 'hledger print -a'.  It is just
a convenience so that you don't have to always declare a type G account,
for simple entries.


